            <h2>
                What Is an EMI?
            </h2>

            <p>
                EMI stands for Equated Monthly Instalment. It is the
                regular amount paid by a borrower toward a loan.
                An EMI generally contains two components: principal
                repayment and interest.
            </p>

            <p>
                In a reducing-balance loan, the proportion of interest
                and principal changes during the repayment period.
                Earlier EMIs generally contain more interest because
                the outstanding principal is higher.
            </p>


            <h2>
                How Is EMI Calculated?
            </h2>

            <p>
                For a standard reducing-balance loan, EMI can be
                calculated using the following formula:
            </p>

            <div class="notice">
                EMI = P × r × (1 + r)<sup>n</sup> /
                ((1 + r)<sup>n</sup> − 1)
            </div>

            <p>
                Where P is the principal loan amount, r is the monthly
                interest rate and n is the total number of monthly
                instalments.
            </p>


            <h2>
                What Is Reducing Balance Interest?
            </h2>

            <p>
                Under reducing-balance interest, interest is calculated
                using the outstanding principal. When part of the
                principal is repaid, the balance on which future
                interest is calculated becomes smaller.
            </p>


            <h2>
                What Is Flat Rate Interest?
            </h2>

            <p>
                A typical flat-rate calculation applies the quoted
                interest rate to the original principal rather than
                recalculating interest on the declining outstanding
                balance.
            </p>

            <p>
                This means that a flat rate and a reducing rate with
                the same numerical percentage can have very different
                effective borrowing costs.
            </p>


            <h2>
                Does Loan Prepayment Reduce Interest?
            </h2>

            <p>
                For a reducing-balance loan, an earlier reduction in
                principal can reduce future interest because future
                interest is calculated on a smaller outstanding balance.
            </p>

            <p>
                However, borrowers should check the loan agreement for
                part-prepayment limits, minimum amounts, lock-in periods,
                processing fees, taxes and applicable foreclosure charges.
            </p>


            <h2>
                What Is Loan Foreclosure?
            </h2>

            <p>
                Foreclosure means closing a loan before its original
                scheduled maturity. The borrower normally pays the
                eligible outstanding amount plus applicable charges
                specified by the lender.
            </p>

            <p>
                The actual foreclosure amount should always be confirmed
                with the lender because the final amount can include
                charges, taxes, interest up to the closure date or other
                contractual amounts.
            </p>


            <h2>
                Why Use an EMI Calculator?
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li>
                    Estimate your monthly EMI before taking a loan.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Calculate total interest over the loan period.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Understand principal and interest in every EMI.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Compare flat and reducing interest calculations.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Estimate your outstanding loan balance.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Calculate potential prepayment savings.
                </li>

                <li>
