        <section class="card">

            <h2>
                Frequently Asked Questions About EMI
            </h2>


            <details>

                <summary>
                    What is an EMI?
                </summary>

                <p>
                    EMI means Equated Monthly Instalment. It is the
                    regular payment made toward a loan and generally
                    consists of principal and interest.
                </p>

            </details>


            <details>

                <summary>
                    What is the difference between flat and reducing interest?
                </summary>

                <p>
                    Flat-rate interest is typically calculated using
                    the original principal, whereas reducing-balance
                    interest is calculated using the outstanding
                    principal. Therefore, the same quoted percentage
                    can produce different total interest costs.
                </p>

            </details>


            <details>

                <summary>
                    Does prepayment reduce EMI?
                </summary>

                <p>
                    It depends on the lender and the option selected.
                    After a prepayment, a lender may reduce the EMI,
                    reduce the remaining tenure, or provide another
                    repayment structure according to the loan agreement.
                </p>

            </details>


            <details>

                <summary>
                    Does keeping the EMI unchanged after prepayment save more interest?
                </summary>

                <p>
                    In many reducing-balance loans, keeping the EMI
                    unchanged can reduce the remaining tenure and
                    therefore reduce future interest. The exact result
                    depends on the lender's repayment rules.
                </p>

            </details>


            <details>

                <summary>
                    What is a loan amortization schedule?
                </summary>

                <p>
                    An amortization schedule is a month-by-month
                    breakdown showing opening balance, EMI, interest,
                    principal repayment, prepayment and closing balance.
                </p>

            </details>


            <details>

                <summary>
                    How is foreclosure amount calculated?
                </summary>

                <p>
                    A simplified estimate can be calculated as the
                    eligible outstanding balance plus the applicable
                    foreclosure charge. Actual lender calculations may
                    include additional contractual charges, taxes or
                    interest up to the closure date.
                </p>

            </details>


            <details>

                <summary>
                    Is the 4% foreclosure charge always applicable?
                </summary>

                <p>
                    No. A 4% value in this calculator is only an input
                    assumption. Actual foreclosure charges depend on
                    the lender, loan product, agreement, timing and
                    applicable rules.
                </p>

            </details>


            <details>

                <summary>
                    Is this EMI calculator accurate for every bank or NBFC?
                </summary>

                <p>
                    The calculator provides mathematical estimates based
                    on the values entered. Actual lender calculations can
                    differ because of daily interest calculations, dates,
                    fees, insurance, taxes, rounding, prepayment rules
                    and other contractual conditions.
                </p>

